WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.285
Who reviews: domain experts, with one accountable owner.

2
00:00:03.565 --> 00:00:11.459
The people best placed to judge an AI system’s outputs are usually the people who know the domain, not the people who know the model.

3
00:00:11.739 --> 00:00:18.651
A leasing assistant should be judged by someone who has run a leasing office; a clinical summarizer by a clinician.

4
00:00:18.931 --> 00:00:31.091
Hamel Husain’s field guide argues that these experts should review outputs directly, and even edit prompts through an admin version of the product, rather than routing every judgment through an engineer.

5
00:00:31.371 --> 00:00:38.219
It also warns against AI jargon when working with them: ask “is this answer acceptable to send to a tenant?

6
00:00:38.499 --> 00:00:40.141
”, not “rate the faithfulness.

7
00:00:40.421 --> 00:00:40.719
”.

8
00:00:40.999 --> 00:00:53.586
For small and medium teams, Husain and Shreya Shankar’s evals FAQ recommends one domain expert as a benevolent dictator: the person who sets the quality bar and makes the final call.

9
00:00:53.866 --> 00:00:55.956
One owner keeps labels consistent.

10
00:00:56.236 --> 00:01:07.991
If you have several reviewers, you need what week 2 taught: have them label the same items independently, measure agreement (Cohen’s kappa), discuss the disagreements, update the rubric, and relabel.

11
00:01:08.271 --> 00:01:16.655
The FAQ discourages outsourcing this error analysis to people outside the team, because the point is to learn what goes wrong in your product.

12
00:01:16.935 --> 00:01:19.367
Two practical rules from the same sources.

13
00:01:19.647 --> 00:01:30.634
Use binary pass or fail judgments with a written critique, not 1 to 5 scales: reviewers struggle to separate adjacent scale points, and the business needs a threshold anyway.

14
00:01:30.914 --> 00:01:39.895
And keep engineers in the room for the first rounds, because the critiques often point at a bug (a retrieval filter, a missing tool) rather than a prompt.

15
00:01:40.175 --> 00:01:43.418
For you as a PM leader, this is a staffing decision.

16
00:01:43.698 --> 00:01:48.540
Name the quality owner, protect their time, and treat their labels as a product asset.
